@General_member_A — a short delay is possible but first check Account > Activity/History for those three items, try Chrome in Incognito (or another browser) to rule out cached views, and confirm the edits were made while signed into the same account; if they still don’t appear, reply here with the three item names and approximate edit timestamps and I’ll escalate with logs. 

@Enthusiast_member_B — if v5.3.2 doesn’t show a “lock to home timezone” toggle that likely means the control is on a staged rollout or feature flag; update to the latest app if available and meantime use geofenced schedules or create a separate “home” schedule you pause while traveling — tell me if you want me to check rollout availability for your account/region. 

@Basic_member_C — to skip one evening: open the app > Smart Schedules > tap your Evening schedule and choose “Skip next occurrence” (or toggle the schedule off), or simply run the manual Scene/thermostat Hold for that night; reply if you don’t see the Skip option and I’ll guide you step‑by‑step.

@General_member_A — I’ve escalated the three items you listed (Kitchen lights – morning scene; Office heater – weekday schedule; Hallway dimmer – evening) with their 9:15–9:30am ET timestamps and app logs; you should get an initial Ops update within 24–48 hours and I’ll post their reply here.  
@Enthusiast_member_B — for travel-friendly mornings either (1) use a fixed clock time (so your routine is the same no matter where you are) or (2) use a geofenced “home” schedule you leave running at home and pause while away; if you want consistent daylight-aware behavior, use sunrise + offset but that will follow local sunrise.  
I can check whether the "lock to home timezone" flag is enabled for your account/region if you want me to.  
@Basic_member_C — on Android 5.3.2 go to App > Smart Schedules > tap Evening schedule > Advanced > Skip next occurrence; if that option isn’t visible, toggle the schedule off now and re-enable it tomorrow morning to skip just tonight without deleting the schedule.  
@Feedback_member_D — thanks for the suggestion; I’ve passed the request for a clearer “travel mode / keep on home time” toggle to Product for prioritization.

@General_member_A — it’s safe to keep making small edits, but avoid deleting those items until Ops confirms; after each change tap Settings > Sync Now and note the new timestamps so we’re able to correlate them with the team.  
@Enthusiast_member_B — rollout windows vary but are usually on the order of weeks rather than months; as a rule try sunrise +15–30 minutes for Boston and sunrise +20–35 minutes for London in winter (or use a fixed clock time if you want identical behavior when you travel), and tell me if you want me to check whether your account/region is flagged for the timezone control.  
@Basic_member_C — if you re-enable the schedule later it will not retroactively run missed events but will resume future occurrences and your schedule history remains intact.  
@General_member_E — to run a weekend-only scene: App > Smart Schedules > Create New > pick your scene/time > set Repeat to Saturday and Sunday only > Save.  
I’ll post any Ops updates here and can check rollout/feature-flag status if you reply with device, app version, and region.

@General_member_A — this was a backend-side issue being fixed now so it appears to be a one‑off; to reduce future risk keep your app updated, tap Settings > Sync Now after edits, avoid simultaneous edits on multiple devices, and save edit timestamps—if those three edits still aren’t visible a few hours after the rollout, reply here with your app’s Last sync timestamp and I’ll re‑escalate.  
@Enthusiast_member_B — glad sunrise+30 worked; when the "lock to home timezone" toggle appears, use it if you want identical clock-time behavior while traveling, but stick with sunrise+offset if you prefer routines that follow local daylight.  
@General_member_E — for a Vacation schedule, give it distinct active days or a geofence and ensure it doesn’t overlap the weekend scene (or disable the weekend scene while testing); use Run Now + the Activity log to confirm it behaves as expected.  
If anyone wants me to check a feature-flag or confirm the sync fix for their account, reply with device, app version, region, and Last sync timestamp.

@General_member_A — background sync is fine for normal use; tap Settings > Sync Now when you need edits to appear immediately (or before switching devices) and check Settings > Last sync to confirm.  
@Enthusiast_member_B — for a daylight feel stick with sunrise+offset, but use the lock-to-home timezone (or a fixed home schedule + geofence) if you want identical clock-times while traveling.  
@General_member_F — common Android causes for missed alerts: system Notification permissions or the app’s notification channel turned off, battery optimization/Doze blocking background activity, or background data restricted; check those, then try clearing the app cache or reinstalling.  
If notifications still fail, reply with a recent Last sync timestamp and confirmation that system + in‑app notifications are enabled and I’ll help escalate.

@General_member_F — scheduled runs should normally send the same push as manual triggers, so check App > Activity to confirm the run entry, see which device/hub executed it (another device may have run it), and look for a “notification sent” line or Android system Notification History; if Activity shows the run but no notification, sign out/in to refresh your push token and, if it recurs, reply with the missed-run timestamp and Last sync so I can escalate.  
@General_member_G — since Duplicate isn’t available, open App > Smart Schedules > your living room schedule > Edit and note each device/action (or take a cropped screenshot), then go to App > Scenes > + Create Scene > add your bedroom devices and enter the same on/off/brightness/thermostat values > Save; finally create the bedroom schedule via App > Smart Schedules > + Create > select your new Scene > set time/days > Save.  
Reply if you want me to walk through the exact taps live.
